From tru64-unix-managers  Thu Jan 05 15:10:22 1995
From: "Dr. Tom Blinn, 603-881-0646" <tpb () zk3 ! dec ! com>
Date: Thu, 05 Jan 1995 15:10:22 +0000
To: tru64-unix-managers
Subject: The need for disktab entries in DEC OSF/1
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=96075316706362

Thanks to Stephane Bortzmeyer for his pointer to the Sun managers list's
disk format information.

It will probably come as no surprise that maintaining such lists is a big
nuisance, especially as more and more quality SCSI disks enter the market.

In DEC OSF/1 V3.2 (due out soon) most of the need for disktab entries will
go away -- the SCSI CAM subsystem (and in particular disklabel) will look at
the device and determine its geometry and other relevant information from
the vendor supplied data.  So, in most cases, you simply won't need to have
a disktab entry for the device.  I *think* most of this is functional in the
V3.0 release as well, but I'm not certain.  We are working to make even more
improvements in the SCSI CAM subsystem in future releases, with addition of 
documentation to make it clear what you can just have work.

So, if you have a new SCSI disk and you don't have the disktab entry, you
can probably get it to work correctly by simply telling disklabel to use a
disk type that isn't listed in disktab, and it will query the device.

Note that you can use the SCU utility (see the scu(8) reference page) to get
information on SCSI devices and display it.  (You can use it for a variety
of other tasks, as well, such as formatting a floppy disk or playing a CD in
a CD reader -- as described in the reference page.)  In many cases, this is
what you'd need to craft your own disktab entry.

